   old=r"""The analytical core of the thesis is the Tri-Lateral Retention Feasibility Model (TRF Model), a constraint-satisfaction formulation developed in Chapter 4 within the established tradition of formal legal-compliance verification (Feng, Marsso, Sabetzadeh, & Chechik, 2023). The model decomposes each cross-border audit record into an event-metadata component, itself divided into the accessor's fields and the link to the patient, and a payload bound by a cryptographic commitment, expresses the three regimes as constraints over discretised time, and examines the satisfiability of the constraint system under two competing semantics of audit verifiability. The model is tri-lateral in its structure, being floor, right, and ceiling, rather than in the number of instruments it accommodates. The retention floor is parameterised, so that the ten-year cross-border audit floor and the one-year Secure Processing Environment log floor are two instances of one constraint rather than two separate constraints. Under plaintext-verifiability semantics, in which a verifiable audit record is one whose payload remains accessible in plaintext, the system is shown to be infeasible whenever an erasure deadline or a deletion-ceiling deadline falls within a record's retention window. Under an alternative semantics, in which verifiability is defined as accountability-preservation (intact metadata, a surviving commitment, and a logged, witnessed invalidation event) the system is shown to be jointly satisfiable for every arrival pattern of erasure requests and permit expiries. The mechanism that realises this feasibility, termed Crypto-Erasure by Invalidation, applies the cryptographic-erasure technique recognised in NIST Special Publication 800-88 Revision 2 at the audit-trail boundary: the key binding a record to its payload is destroyed, while the invalidation event itself is appended to a tamper-evident audit log. The mechanism is thus the constructive witness of the model's feasibility result, not an independently posited design. Two further results locate the conflict: a deletion ceiling collides with a retention floor exactly when it is no longer than the floor, and the event it is anchored to decides whether the collision can be read from the text (Proposition 3); and moving a record out of the regulated environment meets the ceiling but not the right to erasure (Proposition 4).""",
